// Variable IDENTITY in the guarded-formula conversion's unguarded-variable
// check.
//
// HS `remainingUnguarded` (Theory.Constraint.System.Guarded) works over
// `[LVar]`, so its `\\` and `intersect` use `instance Eq LVar` (Term.LTerm) —
// name AND index AND sort.  Two spellings that share only the name are two
// different variables, and a guard that mentions one of them does not cover
// the other.  Every restriction below is closed and existential-free, so each
// carries a `// safety formula` line and none raises a `Formula guardedness`
// warning; conflating any component of that identity drops a binder into the
// unguarded set and answers with a WARNING block instead.
//
//   - `Collision` binds `x.1` under an enclosing `x`.  The guard's right-hand
//     side is the OUTER `x`, which is not itself a binder here and so is
//     covered, and that is what lets the left-hand side guard `x.1` and `z`.
//     `x` is taken by the enclosing binder, so the conversion cannot drop the
//     index and the binder prints as `∀ x.1 z.`;
//   - `NoCollision` is the same shape one letter apart.  `y.1` collides with
//     nothing, so the conversion renames it and the same source prints
//     `∀ y z.` — the index-bearing spelling is not what carries the corner;
//   - `SigillessTimepoint` writes the guard's timepoint without the `#`
//     sigil.  HS parses an action atom's `@`-argument as `LSortNode` whatever
//     the sigil, so `j` there is the binder `#j`; reading it as a message
//     variable leaves `#j` unguarded.
//
// Compared slices: the wf report block AND the `theory … end` echo (see
// check.sh) — the former is the whole assertion for the guardedness check,
// which prints nothing when it passes.

theory GuardedNameCollision
begin

restriction Collision:
  "All x #NOW. Foo(x) @ #NOW ==> (All x.1 z. (<x.1, z> = x) ==> F)"

restriction NoCollision:
  "All x #NOW. Foo(x) @ #NOW ==> (All y.1 z. (<y.1, z> = x) ==> F)"

restriction SigillessTimepoint:
  "All x #i. Foo(x) @ #i ==> (All #j. Bar(x) @ j ==> F)"

rule A:
  [ In(x) ] --[ Foo(x), Bar(x) ]-> [ ]

end
